WEBVTT

00:00:01.605 --> 00:00:54.490
Oxford Bookworms Fact Files London by John Escott Read by Judy Bennett Published and copyright Oxford University Press 2000 Chapter 1 This is London Every year More than 9 million people come from countries all over the world to visit London.

00:00:54.549 --> 00:00:59.430
They go to the theatres and museums.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:07.473
They look at interesting old buildings, many of them hundreds of years old.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:15.969
They sit or walk in the beautiful parks, or have a drink in a pub.

00:01:16.029 --> 00:01:23.966
They go to Oxford Street to look at the shops or to Harrods.

00:01:24.026 --> 00:01:29.650
Two million visitors go to the Tower of London.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:35.853
A million more go to see St Paul's Cathedral.

00:01:35.913 --> 00:01:45.930
Yes, London is a big and beautiful city with lots to see and do.

00:01:45.989 --> 00:01:48.683
But how did it all begin?

00:01:48.703 --> 00:02:18.984
Chapter 2 In the Beginning The name London comes from the Romans.

00:02:19.044 --> 00:02:27.211
There were people living here before they came, but we do not know very much about them.

00:02:27.271 --> 00:02:33.126
The Romans came to England in AD 43.

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:40.012
They built houses and other buildings and made a town next to the River Thames.

00:02:40.072 --> 00:02:44.357
They called the town Londonium.

00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:52.836
They built a bridge over the river and ships came up to Londonium from the sea.

00:02:52.896 --> 00:02:56.919
The town got bigger and bigger.

00:02:56.979 --> 00:03:08.778
Important new buildings went up and you can see some of the Roman city wall today near the Museum of London.

00:03:08.838 --> 00:03:15.661
It was a rich town with about 50,000 people living in it.

00:03:15.721 --> 00:03:24.462
But soon after AD 400, the Romans left Londonium to go back to Rome.

00:03:24.522 --> 00:03:29.514
And nobody lived in the town for many hundreds of years.

00:03:29.574 --> 00:03:33.606
The buildings began to fall down.

00:03:33.665 --> 00:03:41.658
Danish soldiers destroyed more buildings nearly 500 years later.

00:03:41.718 --> 00:03:53.364
King Alfred was King of England then, He got the Danes to leave London, and his men built the town again.

00:03:53.424 --> 00:04:01.890
In 1066 William the Conqueror came to England from France to be king.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:08.125
Soon after, he began to build the Tower of London.

00:04:08.185 --> 00:04:19.108
When Henry VIII was king in 1509, 50,000 people lived in London again.

00:04:19.168 --> 00:04:32.231
By the year 1600, there were 200,000, but a lot of them lived in old and dirty buildings.

00:04:32.291 --> 00:04:39.932
In 1665, a hundred thousand people died from an illness called the Plague.

00:04:39.992 --> 00:04:44.688
This was called the Year of the Great Plague.

00:04:44.748 --> 00:04:54.396
A year later, in 1666, there was a big fire, the fire of London.

00:04:54.456 --> 00:05:00.940
It began in a house in Pudding Lane, near London Bridge.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:07.752
More than a quarter of a million Londoners lost their homes in the fire.

00:05:07.812 --> 00:05:14.315
It destroyed St Paul's Cathedral and 88 other churches.

00:05:14.375 --> 00:05:26.828
but the fire also destroyed most of the worst old buildings, and the new houses that went up after this were better for people to live in.

00:05:26.848 --> 00:05:33.189
A new St Paul's Cathedral was built between 1675 and 1711.

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:48.564
By 1881, more than 3 million people lived in London, Today, more than 6 million people live here.

00:05:48.624 --> 00:05:58.398
There were 8 million in the 1960s, but in the 1970s and 1980s, people moved out of the centre of London.

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:33.804
Chapter 3 Visiting the City A quick and easy way to get to different places in the city is to use an underground train.

00:06:33.864 --> 00:06:39.288
The trains run all day and most of the night.

00:06:39.348 --> 00:06:44.153
Buy your ticket before you get on the train.

00:06:44.213 --> 00:06:58.136
It's better not to make your journey between eight o'clock and ten o'clock in the morning or four o'clock and six o'clock in the evening.

00:06:58.196 --> 00:07:02.080
These are called the rush hours.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:15.520
Thousands of people are going to work or coming home again then, and it is difficult to move or to find a place to sit on the train.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:23.296
You can see much more of London from one of its famous red buses.

00:07:23.356 --> 00:07:33.362
Some special visitors' buses take you to many of the interesting places in the city on one journey.

00:07:33.422 --> 00:07:48.445
It takes about one and a half hours, but you can break your journey and get off and on again at the different places you want to visit.

00:07:48.505 --> 00:07:54.551
London taxis are called black caps.

00:07:54.611 --> 00:08:00.184
Most of them are black, but some are not.

00:08:00.244 --> 00:08:07.048
You can stop one if it has a for higher sign on it.

00:08:07.108 --> 00:08:12.972
The drivers are usually friendly and helpful.

00:08:13.032 --> 00:08:18.794
Why not take a boat trip along the river Thames?

00:08:18.854 --> 00:08:25.433
Boats leave Westminster Pier and Charing Cross Pier.

00:08:25.493 --> 00:08:31.030
and they go to Tower Pier and Greenwich.

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:46.932
Between April and October, you can take a longer boat trip to Hampton Court, about four hours, a beautiful palace in a big park.

00:08:46.992 --> 00:08:53.394
Chapter 4 Some places to go.

00:08:53.434 --> 00:09:08.112
The Queen has her London home at Buckingham Palace.

00:09:08.172 --> 00:09:26.078
It is at the end of the Mall, a long road that begins at Trafalgar Square.

00:09:26.138 --> 00:09:34.860
At half past 11 most mornings, the soldiers at Buckingham Palace change the guard.

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:42.062
It takes about 30 minutes and hundreds of visitors come to watch.

00:09:42.122 --> 00:09:50.814
And in August and September, you can usually visit some of the rooms in the palace.

00:09:50.874 --> 00:09:58.740
But there are always lots of people coming to see them, so be ready to wait.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:04.574
You can also visit the Royal Muse at the Palace.

00:10:04.634 --> 00:10:10.559
This is the home of the Queen's Horses and Coaches.

00:10:10.619 --> 00:10:15.267
The Queen's Gallery is also at the Palace.

00:10:15.328 --> 00:10:19.896
and you can visit it at most times of the year.

00:10:19.956 --> 00:10:25.248
Here you can see pictures from all over the world.

00:10:25.308 --> 00:10:33.934
The Tower of London is now a museum and one of London's most famous buildings.

00:10:33.994 --> 00:10:39.044
More than two million people visit it every year.

00:10:39.104 --> 00:10:47.686
yeoman warders, also called beef-eaters, tell them all about the tower.

00:10:47.746 --> 00:10:56.370
You can see the crown jewels and visit the bloody tower and the white tower.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:07.149
Or take a walk round the wall and perhaps see one of the tower's famous black birds, the ravens.

00:11:07.209 --> 00:11:12.326
Tower Bridge is near the Tower of London.

00:11:12.387 --> 00:11:22.035
It is one of the most famous bridges in the city and first opened in 1894.

00:11:22.095 --> 00:11:28.660
St Paul's Cathedral is not far away on Ludget Hill.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:34.898
It was built by Sir Christopher Wren after the fire of London.

00:11:34.958 --> 00:11:40.153
Ren built more than 50 London churches.

00:11:40.213 --> 00:11:56.736
Visitors can go up to the Golden Gallery to look across London.

00:11:56.796 --> 00:12:21.300
Chapter 5 Westminster Westminster Abbey is more than 900 years old and is a very famous London church.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:31.422
After William the Conqueror, every king and queen of England was crowned king or queen here.

00:12:31.482 --> 00:12:40.902
The Houses of Parliament are near Westminster Abbey This is the home of the British government.

00:12:40.962 --> 00:12:47.176
The clock high up on the building is called Big Ben.

00:12:47.236 --> 00:12:55.400
The Prime Minister, the head of the British government, lives at 10 Downing Street.

00:12:55.459 --> 00:13:07.909
Walk along Parliament Street to Whitehall, past the government buildings and you can see the little street on your left.

00:13:07.969 --> 00:13:12.295
parks and gardens.

00:13:12.355 --> 00:13:22.854
When you are tired of looking at buildings, you can sit or walk in one of London's beautiful parks.

00:13:22.914 --> 00:13:33.600
Hyde Park has a lake in the middle called the Serpentine and you can take a boat out on the water.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:40.792
It is a good place to get away from the crowds and the noise of the city.

00:13:40.852 --> 00:13:48.126
You can listen to the speakers at speakers corner near Marble Arch.

00:13:48.186 --> 00:13:53.210
People from all over the world come and speak here.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:14:05.077
You can ask a speaker some difficult questions if you like or you can stand on a box and speak to some of the listeners.

00:14:05.137 --> 00:14:11.080
Kensington Gardens is next to Hyde Park.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:21.804
Here you can see the statue of Peter Pan, the famous boy in the children's story, Peter Pan, by J.M.

00:14:21.844 --> 00:14:23.566
Barry.

00:14:23.626 --> 00:14:29.258
Regent's Park is the home of London Zoo.

00:14:29.318 --> 00:14:36.074
The zoo has thousands of birds and animals from all over the world.

00:14:36.134 --> 00:14:40.219
There is also a theatre in the park.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:52.098
On a summer's evening you can sit out under the night sky and watch a play by William Shakespeare, England's most famous writer.

00:14:52.158 --> 00:14:56.960
St James's Park is next to the mall.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:03.029
It is smaller, but many people think it is more beautiful.

00:15:03.089 --> 00:15:38.714
Chapter 6 Shops and Eating The most famous shop in London, some people say the most famous in the world, is Harrods in Knightsbridge.

00:15:38.774 --> 00:15:42.986
It opened in 1849.

00:15:43.046 --> 00:16:14.281
Oxford Street has many big shops, Selfridges, Marks and Spencer, John Lewis, Debenhams, There are always lots of people looking at the shops here, but at Christmas thousands more people come to see the wonderful Christmas lights and to buy things for their friends and family for Christmas.

00:16:14.341 --> 00:16:19.392
Charing Cross is famous for its book shops.

00:16:19.452 --> 00:16:25.649
There are lots of them and they sell old and new books.

00:16:25.709 --> 00:16:31.338
One of the oldest and most famous is foils.

00:16:31.398 --> 00:16:40.884
It has thousands of books, but it can sometimes be very difficult to find the book you want.

00:16:40.945 --> 00:16:55.216
Covent Garden was once a big food market, but now it has lots of small shops and cafes, and there are street performers to watch.

00:16:55.276 --> 00:17:00.150
There are two very famous markets in London.

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:13.441
Petticoat Lane Market, open on Sundays, is in Middlesex Street and is a good place to buy cheap clothes and things for the home.

00:17:13.501 --> 00:17:27.961
At the market in Portobello Road, open on Saturdays, you can buy old clocks, old chairs and tables and hundreds of other things.

00:17:28.022 --> 00:17:55.922
You can find food from nearly every country in the world in London, in Soho, in the West End of London, C-Map on pages 18 and 19, You can eat food from Italy, India, China, Japan, Greece and lots more places.

00:17:55.982 --> 00:18:00.794
There are also thousands of pubs in the city.

00:18:00.855 --> 00:18:05.827
In many pubs you can eat as well as drink.

00:18:05.886 --> 00:18:10.168
Or why not have some English fish and chips?

00:18:10.228 --> 00:18:13.658
They are cheap and good to eat.

00:18:13.719 --> 00:18:22.032
or you can have tea at the ritz in Piccadilly, or at the Savoy Hotel in the Strand.

00:18:22.092 --> 00:18:49.894
Chapter 7 going out, theatres and music.

00:18:49.954 --> 00:18:59.378
London's West End has some of the best theatres in the world, so tickets can be expensive.

00:18:59.438 --> 00:19:03.919
Go in the afternoon, it is often cheaper.

00:19:03.979 --> 00:19:07.060
There is something for everybody.

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:15.411
From a play by the Royal Shakespeare Company at the Barbican Theatre to Agatha Christie's The Mouse Trap.

00:19:15.471 --> 00:19:23.413
This play began in 1952, and thousands of visitors see it every year.

00:19:23.473 --> 00:19:26.904
There are lots of cinemas to visit.

00:19:26.964 --> 00:19:35.426
The most expensive are in the West End, but you can sometimes get cheap tickets on Mondays.

00:19:35.486 --> 00:19:56.822
You can hear wonderful music and singing from all over the world at the Royal Opera House in Covent Garden, the Royal Albert Hall in Kensington Gore, the London Coliseum in St Martin's Lane and the Barbican Centre Silk Street.

00:19:56.882 --> 00:20:05.920
For ballet, go to the Sadler's Wells Theatre in Rosebury Avenue or to the Royal Opera House.

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:12.864
to get cheap tickets by them an hour or two before it begins.

00:20:12.924 --> 00:20:17.536
Do you like to listen to jazz musicians?

00:20:17.596 --> 00:20:33.618
You can hear some of the best at Ronnie Scott's Club in Frith Street or at the Bull's Head Barns at Barns Bridge or at 100 Club at 100 Oxford Street.

00:20:33.678 --> 00:20:38.069
sport.

00:20:38.129 --> 00:20:43.564
Some of the most famous English football clubs are in London.

00:20:43.624 --> 00:21:20.475
You can see Arsenal play at Arsenal Stadium, Avernal Road, N5, Chelsea play at Stamford Bridge, Fulham Road, SW6 and Tottenham Hotspur play at White Heart Lane, to watch the very English game of cricket go to Lord's Cricket Ground, in St John's Wood, NW-8.

00:21:20.535 --> 00:21:25.800
This is the home of cricket for most English people.

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:47.142
Chapter 8 Museums and Galleries The British Museum in Great Russell Street is the biggest museum in Britain.

00:21:47.202 --> 00:21:59.764
Tickets are free.

00:21:59.824 --> 00:22:08.796
The Museum of London, at 150 London Wall, is one of the most interesting museums in the city.

00:22:08.856 --> 00:22:13.348
It tells the story of London and its people.

00:22:13.408 --> 00:22:34.854
One more museum that tells a story is the Museum of the Moving Image, Momi, on the South Bank, under Waterloo Bridge, This tells the story of cinema and television and there are many things for visitors to see and do.

00:22:34.914 --> 00:22:41.517
You can act with actors on a film set or you can read the news on TV.

00:22:41.537 --> 00:22:49.812
4 million people visit the National Gallery in Trafalgar Square every year.

00:22:49.872 --> 00:22:53.964
They come to look at more than 2000 pictures.

00:22:54.024 --> 00:22:56.472
Tickets are free.

00:22:56.532 --> 00:23:04.360
At the National Portrait Gallery in St Martin's Place, you can see pictures of famous people.

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:07.892
Tickets are free here, too.

00:23:07.952 --> 00:23:14.293
The London dungeon in Touly Street is a museum of horror.

00:23:14.353 --> 00:23:22.776
Half a million people visit it every year, but they don't always stay to see it all.

00:23:22.836 --> 00:23:30.586
Madam to Swords in Marleban Road, is famous for its people made from wax.

00:23:30.646 --> 00:23:36.657
You can see famous people from the past and famous people of today.

00:23:36.717 --> 00:23:59.794
Abraham Lincoln, Mahatma Gandhi, Nelson Mandela, Pavarotti, Marilyn Monroe, Charlie Chaplin, Sylvester Stallone, and in the chamber of horrors, You can see some very bad people.

00:23:59.854 --> 00:24:27.104
Chapter 9 Places and parades the city.

00:24:27.164 --> 00:24:36.498
Londoners often talk about the city, C-Map on pages 18 and 19.

00:24:36.558 --> 00:24:46.834
They are talking about the oldest part of London, the home of the Bank of England and many other big offices.

00:24:46.894 --> 00:24:55.706
About 5,000 people live in the city and at weekends it feels empty.

00:24:55.766 --> 00:25:05.012
but between Monday and Friday, nearly half a million people come here to work in the banks and offices.

00:25:05.072 --> 00:25:11.256
Look for the city men with their dark suits and umbrellas.

00:25:11.316 --> 00:25:22.920
St Paul's Cathedral is in the middle of the city, and the Bank of England has an interesting museum that you can visit.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:33.952
Also in the city is a very tall building, 60.6 metres high, called the Monument.

00:25:34.012 --> 00:25:45.421
Christopher Wren built this too, and it stands on the place where the fire of London began in 1666.

00:25:45.481 --> 00:25:51.185
Some interesting and exciting days.

00:25:51.246 --> 00:26:02.837
Every year On a Saturday morning in June, foot guards and horse guards have a parade for the Queen.

00:26:02.897 --> 00:26:06.778
This is called Trooping the Colour.

00:26:06.838 --> 00:26:11.720
The colour is the flag that the soldiers carry.

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:19.948
Thousands of people stand in the mall to see the Queen and the soldiers go past.

00:26:20.008 --> 00:26:28.274
The exciting, knotting hill carnival is on the last Sunday and Monday in August.

00:26:28.334 --> 00:26:36.918
There are two wonderful parades to watch, one on Sunday and one on Monday.

00:26:36.978 --> 00:26:45.290
And you can see them going through the streets near Portobello Road and Ladbrook Grove.

00:26:45.350 --> 00:26:55.700
On the second Saturday in November, Londoners can see their new Lord Mayor in the Lord Mayor's show.

00:26:55.760 --> 00:27:02.895
A parade from Manchin House, the Lord Mayor's home, to the Strand.

00:27:02.955 --> 00:27:09.820
The Lord Mayor is the most important person in the city after the Queen.

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:16.341
The first Mayor of London was Henry Fitz Allen in 1189.

00:27:16.401 --> 00:27:23.663
They were not called Lord Mares until the time of King Henry VIII.

00:27:23.723 --> 00:27:38.416
Big red buses, London policemen, Buckingham Palace, Speakers Corner, Big Ben, Notting Hill Carnival.

00:27:38.476 --> 00:27:43.269
These are some of the things you can find in London.

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:46.342
But there are many, many more.

00:27:46.402 --> 00:27:47.563
Come and see.

